{"name":"New Navy Blue","hex":"#3b4a55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"new","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Newar."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Recently made, or created.","Of recent origin; having taken place recently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/new"},{"term":"navy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A country's entire maritime military force, including ships and personnel.","A governmental department in charge of a country's maritime military force."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the dark blue colour of navy blue.","Belonging to the navy; typical of the navy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/navy"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Navy blue","blocks":[{"text":"Navy blue, also known simply as navy, is a dark shade of the color blue. The name navy blue originally referred to the color of uniforms worn by officers in the Royal Navy. In the late 18th century, the Royal Navy adopted the color for its sailors' uniforms, partly due to the practical reason that dark colors were less prone to showing dirt and wear during long sea voyages. The color became so associated with naval service that it came to be known simply as \"navy blue.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Flickr_-_Official_U.S._Navy_Imagery_-_The_Blue_Angels_fly_in_formation..jpg/330px-Flickr_-_Official_U.S._Navy_Imagery_-_The_Blue_Angels_fly_in_formation..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navy_blue","wikidata":"Q5975887"},{"title":"History of the United States Navy","blocks":[{"text":"The history of the United States Navy divides into two major periods: the \"Old Navy\", a small but respected force of sailing ships that became notable for innovation in the use of ironclads during the American Civil War, and the \"New Navy\" the result of a modernization effort that began in the 1880s and made it the largest in the world by 1943."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Thomas_Birch_-_Engagement_between_the_%22United_States%22_and_the_%22Macedonian%22.jpg/330px-Thomas_Birch_-_Engagement_between_the_%22United_States%22_and_the_%22Macedonian%22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_United_States_Navy","wikidata":"Q1517192"},{"title":"Navy","blocks":[{"text":"A navy, naval force, military maritime fleet, war navy, or maritime force is the branch of a state's armed forces principally designated for naval and amphibious warfare; namely, lake-borne, riverine, littoral, or ocean-borne combat operations and related functions. It includes anything conducted by surface ships, amphibious ships, submarines, and seaborne aviation, as well as ancillary support, communications, training, and other fields."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/The_Hermione_being_escorted_by_the_USS_Mitscher_%28DDG-57%29_%281%29.jpg/330px-The_Hermione_being_escorted_by_the_USS_Mitscher_%28DDG-57%29_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navy","wikidata":"Q4508"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Blue_with_deep_sky.webp/500px-Blue_with_deep_sky.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Blue_with_deep_sky.webp/500px-Blue_with_deep_sky.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_with_deep_sky.webp"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Wertheimer_Optimist_%2812%29.jpg/500px-Wertheimer_Optimist_%2812%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Wertheimer_Optimist_%2812%29.jpg/500px-Wertheimer_Optimist_%2812%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWertheimer_Optimist_(12).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Navy_Blue_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4159302.jpg/500px-Navy_Blue_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4159302.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Navy_Blue_-_geograph.org.uk_